.TH LIBPTYTEST 7 2026-10-03 "libptytest 0.1" "libptytest Manual"
.SH NAME
libptytest \- architecture of the PTY-driven, cell-exact terminal test library
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B libptytest
tests interactive terminal programs the way a user meets them: the real
binary runs inside a pseudo-terminal, receives real keystrokes, and
everything it writes is turned into a grid of cells by a built-in terminal
emulator.
A test then asks which character, attribute and colour sits in a given
cell, or compares the whole screen with an expected picture.
.PP
The emulator is a
.I test instrument, not a mock of the product:
nothing of the program under test is replaced.
It does stand in for a terminal, so its limits are limits of the tests
written with it.
They are listed under
.B "EMULATOR"
below, and every sequence listed there as understood has a unit test that
fails if it is mis-handled.
.SH ARCHITECTURE
The library has four parts, one source file each, under one public
header,
.IR ptytest.h :
.TP
.B Session (pty_session.c)
Owns the pseudo-terminal and the child process.
.BR pty_run (3)
calls
.BR forkpty (3),
sets up the child's environment and signal dispositions, and
.BR execvp (3)s
the program.
A close-on-exec pipe reports a failed exec back to the parent, so a missing
binary fails at
.BR pty_run (3)
instead of as a later, misleading timeout.
This layer also implements input
.RB ( pty_send (3)),
window size changes
.RB ( pty_resize (3)),
termination
.RB ( pty_close (3))
and every read-only question about the screen
.RB ( pty_cell_text (3),
.BR pty_row_text (3)).
.TP
.B Comparison (pty_compare.c)
Compares a region of the screen with an expected picture of text or of
styles, and writes the report that explains a difference.
It only reads the screen.
.TP
.B Synchronisation (pty_sync.c)
Reads the master side of the PTY and feeds the bytes to the emulator.
Every wait is a wait for a
.I condition
with a deadline \(em text on the screen, an arbitrary predicate, a quiet
period, the child's exit \(em never a fixed sleep
.RB ( pty_wait_for (3),
.BR pty_wait_exit (3)).
While waiting to write input, output is drained, so neither side can fill
its buffer and block the other.
.TP
.B Emulator (pty_screen.c)
A byte-driven state machine (ground, ESC, CSI, OSC and string states, plus
a UTF-8 decoder) whose state survives between reads, so a sequence split
across two reads of the PTY is assembled instead of dropped.
It updates a grid of cells, each holding a UTF-8 string, an attribute
bitmask, a foreground and a background colour, and a width.
Queries the program sends to its terminal (cursor position, device
attributes) are answered through the session, as a real terminal would
answer them.
.PP
Data flows in one direction:
.PP
.EX
  test ──pty_send()──► master ══ PTY ══ slave ──► program under test
                                                         │
  test ◄─pty_cell_*()── screen ◄─emulator◄─ master ◄─────┘
                                   │
                                   └─ answers (DSR, DA) ─► master
.EE
.PP
The shared library exports only the functions declared in
.IR ptytest.h ;
the emulator's internal interface is reachable only from the static
library, which is what this project's own unit tests link.
.SH WRITING A TEST
.IP 1. 4
Open a session with the terminal size the view is designed for, set the
child's environment with
.BR pty_setenv (3)
(at least
.BR HOME ,
and
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ,
.BR XDG_DATA_HOME
and
.BR XDG_CACHE_HOME ,
which override it), and start the program with
.BR pty_run (3).
.IP 2. 4
Wait for what the screen must show before looking at it: for the exact
picture of the view or of its changing part with
.BR pty_wait_region (3)
.RB ( ASSERT_WAIT_SCREEN ,
.BR ASSERT_WAIT_REGION ),
which a half-drawn view or the previous view cannot satisfy; or, for a
single fact, for its text with
.BR pty_wait_for (3).
.IP 3. 4
Compare every view at least once as a whole with
.BR pty_screen_compare (3)
.RB ( ASSERT_SCREEN_EQ ),
and the parts that change between steps as regions with
.BR pty_region_compare (3)
.RB ( ASSERT_REGION_EQ ),
labelled so the report names them, with a wildcard for what the test
cannot know (a clock, a size).
"The text appears somewhere" checks content, not layout: a column shifted by
one, a misplaced border and a truncated line all pass it.
State the styles that carry meaning \(em the selection, the error, the
title \(em as a style map with
.BR pty_region_compare_style (3)
.RB ( ASSERT_REGION_STYLE ).
.IP 4. 4
For a program that finishes by itself, assert its exit status with
.BR pty_wait_exit (3)
before reading files it wrote.
.BR pty_close (3)
terminates a running child, which can cut it off mid-write.
.IP 5. 4
Use
.B ASSERT_SCREEN_NOT_CONTAINS
only on a screen that has something on it; the macro fails on a blank
screen, because an absence assertion that passes on no output also passes
when the program crashed.
.PP
See
.BR pty_assert (3)
for the assertion macros, and the example under
.I examples/
in the source tree.
.SH READING A FAILURE
A failed comparison is meant to be understood from the log alone.
Its report shows the differing rows as expected (\(oq\-\(cq) and actual
(\(oq+\(cq) under a ruler of screen columns, with carets under every
differing cell and unchanged rows around them; then notes that classify
the difference \(em moved to another row, moved as a block, shifted left
or right (the whole row or one stretch), truncated, extra text, blank,
letter case, a different kind of blank \(em and spell out the first
differing cell in code points and style.
See
.BR pty_region_compare (3)
for an example.
.PP
When more is needed:
.BR pty_screen_dump (3)
prints the whole screen framed,
.BR pty_screen_dump_ansi (3)
saves it with its colours (and loads back with
.BR pty_feed (3)),
.BR pty_cell_describe (3)
explains one cell, and
.B PTYTEST_SNAPSHOT=1
makes the assertion macros print the actual region as a C literal.
.PP
With
.B PTYTEST_EVIDENCE_DIR
set, every failed assertion about a session leaves its evidence in that
directory \(em report, screen as text, screen with colours \(em named
after the file, function and line of the assertion
.RB ( pty_save_evidence (3)),
for a CI job to keep with the failed run.
.SH EMULATOR
The emulator implements the subset of xterm that programs use under
.BR TERM=xterm\-256color ,
which is what the child is given by default.
.SS Understood
.TP
.B C0 controls
BS, HT, LF, VT, FF (as LF), CR, SO, SI; CAN and SUB abort a sequence.
.TP
.B ESC sequences
DECSC/DECRC (ESC 7, ESC 8), IND (ESC D), NEL (ESC E), RI (ESC M),
HTS (ESC H), RIS (ESC c), DECALN (ESC # 8), designation of G0 and G1 as
ASCII or DEC special graphics (ESC ( 0, ESC ( B, ESC ) 0).
.TP
.B Cursor movement
CUU, CUD, CUF, CUB, CNL, CPL, CHA, HPA, HPR, VPA, VPR, CUP, HVP, CHT,
CBT; CUU and CUD stop at the scrolling margins.
.TP
.B Editing
ED 0/1/2/3, EL 0/1/2, ECH, ICH, DCH, IL, DL, SU, SD, REP; insert mode
(IRM, SM/RM 4).
Erased cells take the current background colour (background colour
erase), as xterm does.
.TP
.B Scrolling
DECSTBM scrolling region; LF, IND and RI scroll within it; origin mode
(DECOM).
.TP
.B SGR
0 reset; 1 bold; 2 dim; 3 italic; 4 underline (also 4:0 off and 4:n
styles, reported as underline); 5 and 6 blink; 7 reverse; 8 invisible;
9 strike-through; 21 double underline (reported as underline); 22, 23, 24,
25, 27, 28, 29 resets; 30\(en37, 90\(en97 foreground; 40\(en47, 100\(en107
background; 38 and 48 with 5;n or 2;r;g;b, in the semicolon and the colon
forms; 39 and 49 default colours; 58 (underline colour) is parsed and
dropped.
.TP
.B Modes (DECSET/DECRST)
1 application cursor keys (which
.BR pty_send_key (3)
follows), 6 origin, 7 autowrap, 25 cursor visible, 47, 1047, 1048 and
1049 alternate screen and saved cursor.
.TP
.B Tab stops
Every eighth column by default; HTS, TBC 0 and 3.
.TP
.B Answers
DSR 5 (status), DSR 6 (cursor position report), DA1 (as a VT220 with ANSI
colour) and DA2.
.TP
.B Text
UTF-8, with invalid and truncated sequences shown as U+FFFD.
Character width comes from the C library's
.BR wcwidth (3)
in a UTF-8 locale, the function the program under test most likely uses:
wide characters take two cells, combining marks join the preceding cell.
Deferred wrap at the right margin behaves as on a real terminal: the
cursor stays in the last column until the next printable character, and
SGR does not cancel it.
.SS Ignored
These are consumed without effect, so they never appear as text, but they
change nothing a test can observe:
.IP \(bu 2
OSC strings (window title, hyperlinks, colour queries \(em which are
therefore not answered), DCS (including sixel graphics), SOS, PM and APC.
.IP \(bu 2
Mouse reporting, focus events, bracketed paste, keypad application mode
(ESC = and ESC >), cursor style (DECSCUSR), window operations (CSI t),
modifyOtherKeys (CSI > m) and other private-marker sequences, the kitty
keyboard protocol, C1 controls encoded in UTF-8.
.SS Not emulated
.IP \(bu 2
No scrollback: what scrolls off the top is gone.
.IP \(bu 2
No reflow on resize: content keeps its top-left position and is clipped.
.IP \(bu 2
No left/right margins (DECSLRM), double-width or double-height lines
(DECDWL, DECDHL), or character sets other than ASCII and DEC special
graphics.
.IP \(bu 2
Blinking, invisible and underline styles are attributes only; no
rendering is implied.
.PP
A program that depends on any of these shows a difference between this
emulator and a real terminal, and a test cannot catch a fault in it.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PTYTEST_EVIDENCE_DIR
Directory where failed assertions save their evidence; see
.BR pty_save_evidence (3).
.TP
.B PTYTEST_SNAPSHOT
When set, a failed
.B ASSERT_SCREEN_EQ
or
.B ASSERT_REGION_EQ
also prints the actual region as a C literal.
.TP
.B PTYTEST_CLOSE_GRACE_MS
Default for
.BR pty_set_close_grace (3):
how long
.BR pty_close (3)
lets a running child exit by itself before signalling it.
Set it when the program under test is built with coverage
instrumentation, whose data files are written during exit and are left
truncated by a signal arriving at that moment.
.SH LINKING
.EX
cc \-o my\-test my\-test.c $(pkg\-config \-\-cflags \-\-libs libptytest)
.EE
.PP
or, with CMake:
.PP
.EX
find_package(ptytest 0.1 REQUIRED)
target_link_libraries(my\-test PRIVATE ptytest::ptytest)
.EE
.PP
The source tree can also be used with
.BR add_subdirectory() ,
which builds only the library.
